hRatio":0.48085},{"ratio":0.87,"watchRatio":0.47615},{"ratio":0.88,"watchRatio":0.4712},{"ratio":0.89,"watchRatio":0.46699999999999997},{"ratio":0.9,"watchRatio":0.46515},{"ratio":0.91,"watchRatio":0.45840000000000003},{"ratio":0.92,"watchRatio":0.4538},{"ratio":0.93,"watchRatio":0.4476},{"ratio":0.94,"watchRatio":0.44494999999999996},{"ratio":0.95,"watchRatio":0.43979999999999997},{"ratio":0.96,"watchRatio":0.43235},{"ratio":0.97,"watchRatio":0.4235},{"ratio":0.98,"watchRatio":0.41125},{"ratio":0.99,"watchRatio":0.39725},{"ratio":1,"watchRatio":0.3913}],"script":{"source_path":"all-146-tagged.json","words":144,"wpm":null,"lang":"en","chapters":[],"blocks":[],"text":"You think powerful sultans only care about heads full of women?  You've been reading the wrong history books.  From the Khiljis to the Mughals.  Here are three popular rulers who prove history is rarely straight.  Take the brutal Alauddin Khilji.  He conquered India but he was conquered by his own slave General Malik Kafur.  From the chronicle itself, Was Deeply in Love.  Trusting Kuffur with his empire and his sanity.  Then there is Ali Adilsha.  He barely looked at his wives. Instead he surrounded himself with bodyguards of beautiful young men.  Fat the Wildest One Power, the First Muggle Apparition. In his own diary, he admits to having a massive crush on a boy named Baburi from the Bazaar.  He writes about wandering the streets barefoot, just hoping to catch a glimpse of him.  It turns out history is as straight as you think."}}